WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:14.580
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We turn now to item six on
the agenda, which is the paper relating to

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:20.320
a healthy NHS workforce. Ian, will you present
the paper?

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.940
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you very much, Chair. The
forward view committed us to getting serious

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:33.830
about prevention. Employers have a critical
role in that - employers in society at large,

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:41.870
not just in the NHS - but if we are going
to encourage a wider drive here, we need to

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:47.769
focus on what is within our gift within the
NHS in the first instance, and that is exactly

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:56.479
what this programme is about. We have three
aspects to it. We have our plan to develop

00:00:56.479 --> 00:01:05.820
a nationally specified occupational health
service for GPs; with PHE, a drive to raise

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:14.210
the standards of food and nutrition in the
NHS; and then thirdly a major new programme

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:23.960
to improve NHS staff health where we have
selected ten NHS partner organisations who,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:30.299
in a style and approach analogous to the way
we are working with our vanguard sites for

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:40.020
new care models, will work as a group to develop
a core recipe of replicable approaches that

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:47.920
are then capable of wider spread and that
they can of course add to. I commend this

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.950
programme to the Board.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:54.689
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Questions and comments, please.

00:01:54.689 --> 00:02:05.389
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: It seems to me that obviously
we are an employer, and it is right, as you

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:10.920
said, that the five-year forward view committed
us to being better at these aspects of being

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.610
an employer. However, we are also an initiator
and supporter to other bits which are employers

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:24.890
in their own right, as it were. I just wonder
where the overlap is with Public Health England.

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.910
Is it doing more in lots of other spaces with
lots of other employers? I am thinking about

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:34.640
social care again, as you might imagine. We
are always talking about health and social

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:41.420
care, but social care is always the poor relation
in this. Is there something, for example,

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:48.400
that Public Health is doing, so that I can
be more relaxed? It is not NHS England's kind

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.510
of territory, but it is very appropriate for
them.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:03:00.910
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Your point is very well made,
Moira. There is both the challenge around

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:12.500
the wider care system, not just the NHS, and
what we do with employers more widely. We

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:21.190
are acutely mindful of both of those elements.
This programme - this first step - is a narrower

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:29.470
focus on how we work with NHS employers. We
have been developing this with PHE and the

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:37.060
other national arm's-length bodies as a national
programme. I see this as the first step, rather

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:41.440
than the last word. Clearly the challenge
for us is how we build on this and spread

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.040
it more widely.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:51.010
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Big picture: because we have
a tax-funded health service, we neglect the

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:56.110
workplace as a place for health improvement.
It is a good thing that we have a tax-funded

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:01.430
health service, but it does mean that for
the 30-odd million working-age adults, despite

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:07.290
the fact that since 1998 we have been talking
about workplace health settings, not very

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:13.020
much has actually happened. It is hard for
the NHS to become an effective advocate and

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:17.630
partner until we have begun to put our own
house in order, hence the initiatives that

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:22.490
Ian has described. To your broader question,
Moira, there is frankly a lot more that we

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:29.629
want to do with employers, unions and businesses,
many of which are running excellent programmes

00:04:29.629 --> 00:04:32.530
in their own particular settings.

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:38.120
So the debate that we are having with some
of the big employer's organisations, the TUC

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:44.189
and others is about how we really put those
kind of approaches on a much broader footing,

00:04:44.189 --> 00:04:53.710
particularly for employee groups at greatest
risk. Paradoxically, it is often white-collar

00:04:53.710 --> 00:05:01.430
employers who are most expansive in their
offers in this space. We also have to look

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:07.060
at retail, manual occupations, hospitality
and various other places as well. There is

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.659
quite a bit of work going on behind the scenes
on what this might look like, including whether

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:17.949
we could even contemplate fiscal incentives
in some regard for credentialed workplace

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:22.029
health programmes, for which there is quite
a good evidence base now, and not just around

00:05:22.029 --> 00:05:29.560
some of the mental health and stress-related
aspects of this or MSK, but other pieces as

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.060
well.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.810
It is a work in progress, and I think it is
a huge opportunity for us if we look out over

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:38.879
five years. There are things we are doing
to try to connect the health checks programme

00:05:38.879 --> 00:05:43.770
that Public Health England is running work
the workplace and the diabetes prevention

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:50.110
programme that we are kicking off with at-risk
individuals in some of these settings. I think

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:55.180
that just by, frankly, getting the national
conversation going, we are putting more fuel

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:57.050
in the tank on this one.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:05.189
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: That is my question partly
answered. I was just wondering whether organisations

00:06:05.189 --> 00:06:11.930
outside the NHS and in the not-for-profit
sector might join this august list of organisations

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:20.210
that are trying to do this work and learn
with you. I think there are organisations,

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:24.740
not least mine, that would really benefit
from this, and you could benefit from our

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:28.509
learning to date. Why wait?

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:34.759
My other question was more about some sections
of the NHS workforce that appear to have greater

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:39.580
challenges than others in a lot of areas.
That is one of the reasons why we have the

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.620
Workforce Race Equality Standard. We know
there is a direct link. So I was just going

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.650
to ask a question about how we are using that
initiative - which you have led on, Simon,

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:55.219
which is good - to add value to what we know
and how we might intervene in places where

00:06:55.219 --> 00:06:57.330
NHS staff work.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:08.629
&gt;&gt;Ian Dodge: Thank you, Victor. If this programme
is successful, the ten partner organisations

00:07:08.629 --> 00:07:15.360
that we are working with will need to adopt
an inclusive approach. They will need to think

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.409
about how they reach all of the different
staff groups, including people who have typically

00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:33.469
got higher 
levels of stress, grievances and so on. I
think you are right, Victor, to highlight

00:07:33.469 --> 00:07:39.779
the connection between the two initiatives.
Your suggestion and your point about making

00:07:39.779 --> 00:07:46.400
sure that this is not a narrow, tightly drawn,
closed cohort of ten but part of a wider movement

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:53.979
where we can connect to voluntary-sector organisations
and sow the seeds of wider spread, is also

00:07:53.979 --> 00:07:55.789
something that we will need to build in.

00:07:55.789 --> 00:08:04.479
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: There are quite a few. The
City Mental Health Alliance is a good collaboration.

00:08:04.479 --> 00:08:10.639
The Sunday Times and other national papers
run competitions in partnership with employers

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:17.589
on this. Sally Davies has been leading a bunch
of work in this area, as has Carol Black,

00:08:17.589 --> 00:08:22.949
and NHS employers are pretty engaged. We are
definitely less than the sum of our parts

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:28.029
in terms of the action that is happening nationally,
and it has not had the oomph that it needs,

00:08:28.029 --> 00:08:32.960
to this is partly about trying to mobilise
those disparate efforts.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:40.649
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: I just wanted to provide
some assurance to the Board that we are taking

00:08:40.649 --> 00:08:47.750
this very seriously inside NHS England particularly.
Already, in advance of this being announced,

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:57.170
we have quite a lot of work in place with
our Active Workforce campaign, and training

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:03.380
programmes for mental health in the workplace
across the organisation, as well. I just really

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:08.820
wanted to provide you with that reassurance
that we are doing our best to lead the way

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:10.449
in respect of our own staff.

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:14.240
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think ensuring that at
least one of the lifts in Skipton House is

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.380
always out of action is a very powerful starting
point.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:19.100
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: It definitely helps.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.949
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Exactly - and the other two
are the world's slowest lifts.

00:09:21.949 --> 00:09:22.230
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes.

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:27.810
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: Skipton House does actually
have a sign at the lift, recommending walking,

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:30.089
which we do not have here.

00:09:30.089 --> 00:09:39.829
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right. Any further questions
or comments for Ian? No. I think we note the

00:09:39.829 --> 00:09:43.740
paper, but also I think we take it really
seriously. It is something that the Board

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.889
is utterly committed to. If we cannot care
competently and adequately for the health

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:53.980
of our own employees and those of the NHS
more generally, then it speaks volumes about

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:59.420
what we are able to do in the wider community.
This is a real test bed for us. Thank you,

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:00.449
Ian, very much for that

